{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01A3WrwypuC7whQq2H83cpQr","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Brief — 2026-10-01\n2\t\n3\t## The use case (two sentences, from the scout)\n4\tAfter every meet an age-group swim club's coach or parent volunteer types a\n5\trecap onto the club site by hand: who dropped how much time in which event,\n6\tand who reached a new standard (B, BB, A ...). This template turns one\n7\tswimmer's rows from the meet results (entry time and final time per event)\n8\tinto a time-drop card: one card per swimmer per meet, a batch from one file.\n9\t\n10\t## The template\n11\t- id: `@one-a-day/sports/swim-time-drop-card/v1`. Existing pack `sports`\n12\t  (day 8's powerlifting recap lives there); no new pack.\n13\t- title: Swim Time Drop Card\n14\t- kind: image; canvas hint 1080x1080 (it goes into a club news post and a\n15\t  family's phone; square survives both). 1920x1080 and 1080x1920 must work,\n16\t  and the contract's small canvases (640x360, 480x270) must hold.\n17\t- duration: still.\n18\t\n19\tNeighbours read before writing this: `@one-a-day/dev/bench-delta/v1` (rows\n20\twith bars against a baseline; its lesson is below), `@one-a-day/gaming/\n21\tspeedrun-pb-recap/v1` (delta rows), `@one-a-day/sports/powerlifting-meet-\n22\trecap/v1` (one athlete, one meet). The delta row is not new to this repo;\n23\twhat is new is the construct (entry time against swum time, per event, with\n24\ta standard) and the two decisions below.\n25\t\n26\t## The two design decisions\n27\t\n28\t**1. What the bar measures is stated, and it is one of two honest things.**\n29\tThe scout sketched \"a bar whose width is the drop in seconds\". Seconds are\n30\tthe community's own language (\"dropped 6.2 seconds\"), but a card mixes a 50\n31\tand a 200: in seconds the long event always wins, and a strong 50 looks like\n32\tnothing. Day 2 met the same problem and made the ratio the shared channel.\n33\tSo the drop is always PRINTED in seconds, and the bar is drawn either as the\n34\tshare of the entry time dropped (percent) or as seconds. The footnote names\n35\twhich, and names the scale. That choice is the variant pair, and the loser\n36\tstays one prop away (`bar`).\n37\t\n38\tThe full track is this card's largest drop. Bars compare one swimmer's swims\n39\twith each other, not swimmers with each other: the card is for a family, not\n40\ta leaderboard, and a child with small drops still gets one full bar.\n41\t\n42\t**2. A slower swim is printed, not drawn.** It gets a plus sign and its\n43\tnumber in the ordinary ink, an empty track, no red, no warning colour. The\n44\treader is a ten-year-old and their parents. Honest means the number is\n45\tthere; it does not mean the card shouts it.\n46\t\n47\t## Layout (regions, ratios)\n48\t\n49\t```\n50\tsquare / portrait                            landscape\n51\t+----------------------------------------+   +------------------------------------------------------+\n52\t| TESSA MARLOW                   [ SCY ] |   | TESSA MARLOW                               [ SCY ]   |\n53\t| Larkmoor Swim Club                     |   | Larkmoor Swim Club - October Kickoff - Oct 3-4       |\n54\t| October Kickoff Invitational           |   +------------------------------------------------------+\n55\t| Oct 3-4, 2026                          |   | EVENT     ENTRY    FINAL    (bar track)   DROP       |\n56\t+----------------------------------------+   | 50 Free   31.84    30.97    [======    ]  -0.87  BB  |\n57\t| EVENT     ENTRY    FINAL     DROP      |   | 100 Free  1:10.52  1:08.31  [=======   ]  -2.21  BB  |\n58\t| 50 Free   31.84    30.97    -0.87  BB  |   | 200 Free  2:36.40  2:29.85  [==========]  -6.55  B   |\n59\t| [========                            ] |   | 100 Back  1:19.77  1:20.19  [          ]  +0.42      |\n60\t| 100 Free  1:10.52  1:08.31  -2.21  BB  |   | ...                                                  |\n61\t| [==========                          ] |   +------------------------------------------------------+\n62\t| ... (N rows, 1..8)                     |   | 5 of 6 swims faster - 14.82 s dropped                |\n63\t+----------------------------------------+   | vs entry time. Bars: share of the entry time ...     |\n64\t| 5 of 6 swims faster                    |   +------------------------------------------------------+\n65\t| 14.82 s dropped                        |\n66\t| vs entry time. Bars: share of ...      |\n67\t+----------------------------------------+\n68\t```\n69\t\n70\t- Three bands, top to bottom: header (about a fifth of the height), the rows\n71\t  (what the other two leave, at least half), the summary band (about a\n72\t  fifth). These are targets, not pins.\n73\t- Header: the swimmer's name is the largest text on the card; club, meet and\n74\t  the details line under it; the course (`SCY` / `SCM` / `LCM`) as a small\n75\t  chip at the top right, because times in different courses do not compare\n76\t  and the card must say which it is.\n77\t- A thin column-head line (`EVENT`, `ENTRY`, `FINAL`, `DROP`) so nobody has to\n78\t  guess which time is which.\n79\t- One row per swim, in the order given (meet order). Cells: event, entry\n80\t  time, final time, drop, standard chip. Times and drops are right-aligned so\n81\t  the decimal points stack.\n82\t- The bar track: in landscape it is a column between FINAL and DROP; in\n83\t  square and portrait it is a strip under the row's text line, the full\n84\t  width of the row (narrow canvases need the width for the text cells, and\n85\t  the bar gets more resolution there). Every track has the same size and the\n86\t  same left edge; a bar grows from the left.\n87\t- The standard chip sits at the row's right end. A row without a standard\n88\t  keeps the space empty (the columns must not shift between rows).\n89\t- Summary band: the count and the total as the card's second-largest text,\n90\t  then one small footnote line (or two when it wraps) that carries the\n91\t  honesty: `vs entry time` and what the bar means.\n92\t- Few rows: cap the row height (a card with one or two swims must not turn\n93\t  into slabs); the rows group sits at the top of its band.\n94\t\n95\tMechanism: everything is static svg text in the bundled font plus plain\n96\tcolour tiles. No drawtext, no children needed, no media.\n97\t\n98\t## Layout contract (the invariants the build sweeps)\n99\t- every text fits its box: swimmer, club, meet, details, the course chip,\n100\t  the four column heads, each row's event / entry / final / drop (a label per\n101\t  row and cell, since the copy differs), each standard chip, the summary, the\n102\t  footnote.\n103\t- the swimmer's name lives in the top 35% (`within yFrac [0, 0.35]`); the\n104\t  summary and the footnote live in the bottom 35% (`within yFrac [0.65, 1]`).\n105\t- every row has a bar track (presence), every track is at least a fifth of\n106\t  the canvas width (`minWidthFrac: 0.2`), and the tracks are the same size\n107\t  (`equal: \"size\"` relation on the track label).\n108\t- a bar is present for every faster swim. That a slower, equal, NT or DQ row\n109\t  has NO bar is asserted in the test, not in the contract.\n110\t- no pinned fractions: the use case does not ask for one.\n111\t\n112\t## Props (name - type - default - what it changes - required?)\n113\tAll optional; every default is part of the picture.\n114\t\n115\t| prop | type | default | what it changes |\n116\t|---|---|---|---|\n117\t| `swimmer` | string | `\"Tessa Marlow\"` | the header name (invented; not a real swimmer) |\n118\t| `club` | string | `\"Larkmoor Swim Club\"` | second line; empty removes it |\n119\t| `meet` | string | `\"October Kickoff Invitational\"` | third line; empty removes it |\n120\t| `details` | string | `\"Oct 3-4, 2026\"` | free line for date, age group, session; empty removes it |\n121\t| `course` | `\"SCY\"` \\| `\"SCM\"` \\| `\"LCM\"` | `\"SCY\"` | the course chip |\n122\t| `swims` | json list | the six rows below | the data: `[{ event, entry, final, standard? }]`, 1..8 rows |\n123\t| `bar` | `\"percent\"` \\| `\"seconds\"` | variant a: `\"percent\"`; variant b: `\"seconds\"` | what the bar length means, and the footnote with it |\n124\t| `accent` | #rrggbb | builder's pick, a pool blue-green | bars, the course chip, the standard chips (the club's colour) |\n125\t| `preset` | `\"dark\"` \\| `\"light\"` | `\"dark\"` | page and ink |\n126\t| `debugLayout` | boolean | `false` | draws the contract over the render |\n127\t\n128\tOne `swims` row is what a results row gives, nothing more:\n129\t- `event`: string, as the club says it (`\"50 Free\"`, `\"200 IM\"`,\n130\t  `\"100 Breaststroke\"`).\n131\t- `entry`: the entry (seed) time as `\"31.84\"`, `\"1:10.52\"` or `\"19:58.44\"`,\n132\t  or `\"NT\"` / empty for no time (a first swim of that event).\n133\t- `final`: the swum time in the same formats, or `\"DQ\"`.\n134\t- `standard`: optional, up to 4 characters (`\"B\"`, `\"BB\"`, `\"AAAA\"`). The\n135\t  caller supplies it; the template does not compute standards (the tables\n136\t  change by season, age and sex).\n137\t\n138\tRules, not props:\n139\t- Times are parsed to integer hundredths; all arithmetic is integer. Printed\n140\t  back as `SS.hh` under a minute and `M:SS.hh` above. Drop = entry - final,\n141\t  printed with a sign: `-0.87` faster, `+0.42` slower, `0.00` equal. ASCII\n142\t  hyphen-minus.\n143\t- `NT` entry: the entry cell says `NT`, the drop cell says so in words (for\n144\t  example `first swim`), no bar, and the swim is not counted in the summary.\n145\t- `DQ` final: the final cell says `DQ`, no drop, no bar, no chip, not counted.\n146\t- Summary: `<f> of <m> swims faster` where m counts swims that have both\n147\t  times, and `<total> s dropped` where the total is the sum of the drops of\n148\t  the faster swims (slower swims are not netted against it; each is printed\n149\t  on its row). With no comparable swim the band says that instead of `0 of 0`.\n150\t- Fail fast, naming the row and the value: a time that does not parse, a\n151\t  missing `final`, zero rows, more than 8 rows (the message says to split the\n152\t  meet across two cards), a `standard` longer than 4 characters.\n153\t- Names: these are people's names, and yesterday's critique faulted an\n154\t  ASCII-only refusal. Check whether the bundled font draws Latin-1 letters\n155\t  (the glyph-coverage gate is the judge); accept what it draws. If that\n156\t  cannot be settled inside the build, refuse the unsupported character with\n157\t  a clear error (never tofu) and write it down as a weak spot.\n158\t\n159\t## Beats\n160\tNone: a still. The scout called a clip where the bars grow \"the obvious\n161\tvariant\"; it is left as a follow-up on purpose. Changing the kind is not one\n162\tidea different, the card is posted and sent as an image, and the open\n163\tquestion today is what the bar should mean.\n164\t\n165\t## Defaults must show\n166\tA believable meet for a swimmer who does not exist, at 1080x1080:\n167\t\n168\t| event | entry | final | drop | standard |\n169\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n170\t| 50 Free | 31.84 | 30.97 | -0.87 | BB |\n171\t| 100 Free | 1:10.52 | 1:08.31 | -2.21 | BB |\n172\t| 200 Free | 2:36.40 | 2:29.85 | -6.55 | B |\n173\t| 100 Back | 1:19.77 | 1:20.19 | +0.42 | |\n174\t| 50 Fly | 36.10 | 34.92 | -1.18 | B |\n175\t| 200 IM | 2:58.03 | 2:54.02 | -4.01 | |\n176\t\n177\tSummary: `5 of 6 swims faster`, `14.82 s dropped`. A stranger sees a swim\n178\tmeet result for one child: six events, two times each, five drops with bars,\n179\tone honest slower swim, four standard chips.\n180\t\n181\tThe rows were chosen so the two variants disagree visibly: in percent the 50\n182\tFree (2.7%) is a longer bar than the 200 IM (2.3%); in seconds the IM (4.01)\n183\tis more than four times the 50 Free (0.87).\n184\t\n185\tThe defaults name no age group or sex: the chips are illustrative and have\n186\tnot been checked against any season's standards table, so the card must not\n187\timply one.\n188\t\n189\t## Acceptance rubric (the critic scores against this)\n190\t1. Renders at defaults at 1080x1080, 1920x1080 and 1080x1920, exit 0, no\n191\t   error mosaic, and the square still reads to a stranger as a swimmer's\n192\t   meet: events, entry and final times, drops, chips, the summary.\n193\t2. The arithmetic is honest: integer hundredths, drop = entry - final, the\n194\t   summary's count and total as defined above (the defaults give 5 of 6 and\n195\t   14.82). A slower swim shows a plus sign, no bar and no warning colour;\n196\t   NT and DQ rows make no claim. `vs entry time` is on the card; the words\n197\t   \"PB\" and \"best time\" are nowhere on it.\n198\t3. The bar says what the footnote says: bar lengths are proportional to the\n199\t   stated quantity (a unit test checks them against the numbers, within the\n200\t   pixel or two quantization takes), the largest fills its track, all tracks\n201\t   share one size and left edge, and `bar` switches the lengths and the\n202\t   footnote together.\n203\t4. Nothing clips or collides from 1 to 8 rows at the seven contract\n204\t   canvases, with a 30-character swimmer name, `100 Breaststroke`, a distance\n205\t   swim (`19:58.44`, a drop of `-1:02.33`), `AAAA` chips on every row, and\n206\t   emptied club / meet / details lines. One or two rows do not become slabs.\n207\t5. The props are a results row and nothing more: no field a parsed results\n208\t   file would not hold except `standard`. The ship note shows how parsed\n209\t   rows map onto `swims`, and neither it nor the tutorial claims the\n210\t   template reads `.hy3` / `.cl2`.\n211\t\n212\tAlso for the builder (the critic's own line 9 will look): the scout's\n213\tevidence quotes real children by name. The why-tutorial's problem page\n214\tquotes the recaps with the names removed (\"[swimmer] dropped 40 seconds in\n215\tthe 200 Free!\"), following day 8's ruling that a tutorial card does not\n216\tcarry a private person's name.\n217\t\n218\t## Variants worth trying (up to 3, each ONE idea different)\n219\t- a: `bar: \"percent\"`. The bar is the share of the entry time dropped; fair\n220\t  between a 50 and a 200. Footnote along the lines of `vs entry time. Bars:\n221\t  share of the entry time dropped; the longest is 4.2%.`\n222\t- b: `bar: \"seconds\"`. The bar is the seconds dropped, the scout's sketch\n223\t  and the recaps' own unit; the long events dominate. Footnote names the\n224\t  longest in seconds.\n225\t- No c is asked for. If a and b are both clean and there is time, the one\n226\t  further idea worth a render is variant a with each bar's percent printed\n227\t  at the bar's end, to test whether a bar in percent beside a number in\n228\t  seconds confuses without it. Drop it if any label fails to fit.\n229\t\n230\t## What is weak in this plan\n231\t- The template does not read the results file. Someone has to parse `.hy3`\n232\t  (flipturn does) and look up standards before the batch works; the day\n233\t  delivers the card, not the pipeline.\n234\t- Eight rows is a cap, and a three-day championship meet with prelims and\n235\t  finals can exceed it. The error says so; it does not solve it.\n236\t- Relays are out: a relay result has no entry time per swimmer.\n237\t- A bar in percent next to a number in seconds asks the reader to hold two\n238\t  units. The footnote is the only thing that explains it; that is the risk\n239\t  variant b exists to measure.\n240\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3b5f6571-be25-4e81-bc62-381b682f29bf","uuid":"33bd0840-7d54-48a3-8cf5-ea06392e201e","timestamp":"2026-10-01T14:08:52.199Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\src\\m0saic-production\\one-a-day\\journal\\2026-10-01\\20-brief.md","content":"# Brief — 2026-10-01\n\n## The use case (two sentences, from the scout)\nAfter every meet an age-group swim club's coach or parent volunteer types a\nrecap onto the club site by hand: who dropped how much time in which event,\nand who reached a new standard (B, BB, A ...). This template turns one\nswimmer's rows from the meet results (entry time and final time per event)\ninto a time-drop card: one card per swimmer per meet, a batch from one file.\n\n## The template\n- id: `@one-a-day/sports/swim-time-drop-card/v1`. Existing pack `sports`\n  (day 8's powerlifting recap lives there); no new pack.\n- title: Swim Time Drop Card\n- kind: image; canvas hint 1080x1080 (it goes into a club news post and a\n  family's phone; square survives both). 1920x1080 and 1080x1920 must work,\n  and the contract's small canvases (640x360, 480x270) must hold.\n- duration: still.\n\nNeighbours read before writing this: `@one-a-day/dev/bench-delta/v1` (rows\nwith bars against a baseline; its lesson is below), `@one-a-day/gaming/\nspeedrun-pb-recap/v1` (delta rows), `@one-a-day/sports/powerlifting-meet-\nrecap/v1` (one athlete, one meet). The delta row is not new to this repo;\nwhat is new is the construct (entry time against swum time, per event, with\na standard) and the two decisions below.\n\n## The two design decisions\n\n**1. What the bar measures is stated, and it is one of two honest things.**\nThe scout sketched \"a bar whose width is the drop in seconds\". Seconds are\nthe community's own language (\"dropped 6.2 seconds\"), but a card mixes a 50\nand a 200: in seconds the long event always wins, and a strong 50 looks like\nnothing. Day 2 met the same problem and made the ratio the shared channel.\nSo the drop is always PRINTED in seconds, and the bar is drawn either as the\nshare of the entry time dropped (percent) or as seconds. The footnote names\nwhich, and names the scale. That choice is the variant pair, and the loser\nstays one prop away (`bar`).\n\nThe full track is this card's largest drop. Bars compare one swimmer's swims\nwith each other, not swimmers with each other: the card is for a family, not\na leaderboard, and a child with small drops still gets one full bar.\n\n**2. A slower swim is printed, not drawn.** It gets a plus sign and its\nnumber in the ordinary ink, an empty track, no red, no warning colour. The\nreader is a ten-year-old and their parents. Honest means the number is\nthere; it does not mean the card shouts it.\n\n## Layout (regions, ratios)\n\n```\nsquare / portrait                            landscape\n+----------------------------------------+   +------------------------------------------------------+\n| TESSA MARLOW                   [ SCY ] |   | TESSA MARLOW                               [ SCY ]   |\n| Larkmoor Swim Club                     |   | Larkmoor Swim Club - October Kickoff - Oct 3-4       |\n| October Kickoff Invitational           |   +------------------------------------------------------+\n| Oct 3-4, 2026                          |   | EVENT     ENTRY    FINAL    (bar track)   DROP       |\n+----------------------------------------+   | 50 Free   31.84    30.97    [======    ]  -0.87  BB  |\n| EVENT     ENTRY    FINAL     DROP      |   | 100 Free  1:10.52  1:08.31  [=======   ]  -2.21  BB  |\n| 50 Free   31.84    30.97    -0.87  BB  |   | 200 Free  2:36.40  2:29.85  [==========]  -6.55  B   |\n| [========                            ] |   | 100 Back  1:19.77  1:20.19  [          ]  +0.42      |\n| 100 Free  1:10.52  1:08.31  -2.21  BB  |   | ...                                                  |\n| [==========                          ] |   +------------------------------------------------------+\n| ... (N rows, 1..8)                     |   | 5 of 6 swims faster - 14.82 s dropped                |\n+----------------------------------------+   | vs entry time. Bars: share of the entry time ...     |\n| 5 of 6 swims faster                    |   +------------------------------------------------------+\n| 14.82 s dropped                        |\n| vs entry time. Bars: share of ...      |\n+----------------------------------------+\n```\n\n- Three bands, top to bottom: header (about a fifth of the height), the rows\n  (what the other two leave, at least half), the summary band (about a\n  fifth). These are targets, not pins.\n- Header: the swimmer's name is the largest text on the card; club, meet and\n  the details line under it; the course (`SCY` / `SCM` / `LCM`) as a small\n  chip at the top right, because times in different courses do not compare\n  and the card must say which it is.\n- A thin column-head line (`EVENT`, `ENTRY`, `FINAL`, `DROP`) so nobody has to\n  guess which time is which.\n- One row per swim, in the order given (meet order). Cells: event, entry\n  time, final time, drop, standard chip. Times and drops are right-aligned so\n  the decimal points stack.\n- The bar track: in landscape it is a column between FINAL and DROP; in\n  square and portrait it is a strip under the row's text line, the full\n  width of the row (narrow canvases need the width for the text cells, and\n  the bar gets more resolution there). Every track has the same size and the\n  same left edge; a bar grows from the left.\n- The standard chip sits at the row's right end. A row without a standard\n  keeps the space empty (the columns must not shift between rows).\n- Summary band: the count and the total as the card's second-largest text,\n  then one small footnote line (or two when it wraps) that carries the\n  honesty: `vs entry time` and what the bar means.\n- Few rows: cap the row height (a card with one or two swims must not turn\n  into slabs); the rows group sits at the top of its band.\n\nMechanism: everything is static svg text in the bundled font plus plain\ncolour tiles. No drawtext, no children needed, no media.\n\n## Layout contract (the invariants the build sweeps)\n- every text fits its box: swimmer, club, meet, details, the course chip,\n  the four column heads, each row's event / entry / final / drop (a label per\n  row and cell, since the copy differs), each standard chip, the summary, the\n  footnote.\n- the swimmer's name lives in the top 35% (`within yFrac [0, 0.35]`); the\n  summary and the footnote live in the bottom 35% (`within yFrac [0.65, 1]`).\n- every row has a bar track (presence), every track is at least a fifth of\n  the canvas width (`minWidthFrac: 0.2`), and the tracks are the same size\n  (`equal: \"size\"` relation on the track label).\n- a bar is present for every faster swim. That a slower, equal, NT or DQ row\n  has NO bar is asserted in the test, not in the contract.\n- no pinned fractions: the use case does not ask for one.\n\n## Props (name - type - default - what it changes - required?)\nAll optional; every default is part of the picture.\n\n| prop | type | default | what it changes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `swimmer` | string | `\"Tessa Marlow\"` | the header name (invented; not a real swimmer) |\n| `club` | string | `\"Larkmoor Swim Club\"` | second line; empty removes it |\n| `meet` | string | `\"October Kickoff Invitational\"` | third line; empty removes it |\n| `details` | string | `\"Oct 3-4, 2026\"` | free line for date, age group, session; empty removes it |\n| `course` | `\"SCY\"` \\| `\"SCM\"` \\| `\"LCM\"` | `\"SCY\"` | the course chip |\n| `swims` | json list | the six rows below | the data: `[{ event, entry, final, standard? }]`, 1..8 rows |\n| `bar` | `\"percent\"` \\| `\"seconds\"` | variant a: `\"percent\"`; variant b: `\"seconds\"` | what the bar length means, and the footnote with it |\n| `accent` | #rrggbb | builder's pick, a pool blue-green | bars, the course chip, the standard chips (the club's colour) |\n| `preset` | `\"dark\"` \\| `\"light\"` | `\"dark\"` | page and ink |\n| `debugLayout` | boolean | `false` | draws the contract over the render |\n\nOne `swims` row is what a results row gives, nothing more:\n- `event`: string, as the club says it (`\"50 Free\"`, `\"200 IM\"`,\n  `\"100 Breaststroke\"`).\n- `entry`: the entry (seed) time as `\"31.84\"`, `\"1:10.52\"` or `\"19:58.44\"`,\n  or `\"NT\"` / empty for no time (a first swim of that event).\n- `final`: the swum time in the same formats, or `\"DQ\"`.\n- `standard`: optional, up to 4 characters (`\"B\"`, `\"BB\"`, `\"AAAA\"`). The\n  caller supplies it; the template does not compute standards (the tables\n  change by season, age and sex).\n\nRules, not props:\n- Times are parsed to integer hundredths; all arithmetic is integer. Printed\n  back as `SS.hh` under a minute and `M:SS.hh` above. Drop = entry - final,\n  printed with a sign: `-0.87` faster, `+0.42` slower, `0.00` equal. ASCII\n  hyphen-minus.\n- `NT` entry: the entry cell says `NT`, the drop cell says so in words (for\n  example `first swim`), no bar, and the swim is not counted in the summary.\n- `DQ` final: the final cell says `DQ`, no drop, no bar, no chip, not counted.\n- Summary: `<f> of <m> swims faster` where m counts swims that have both\n  times, and `<total> s dropped` where the total is the sum of the drops of\n  the faster swims (slower swims are not netted against it; each is printed\n  on its row). With no comparable swim the band says that instead of `0 of 0`.\n- Fail fast, naming the row and the value: a time that does not parse, a\n  missing `final`, zero rows, more than 8 rows (the message says to split the\n  meet across two cards), a `standard` longer than 4 characters.\n- Names: these are people's names, and yesterday's critique faulted an\n  ASCII-only refusal. Check whether the bundled font draws Latin-1 letters\n  (the glyph-coverage gate is the judge); accept what it draws. If that\n  cannot be settled inside the build, refuse the unsupported character with\n  a clear error (never tofu) and write it down as a weak spot.\n\n## Beats\nNone: a still. The scout called a clip where the bars grow \"the obvious\nvariant\"; it is left as a follow-up on purpose. Changing the kind is not one\nidea different, the card is posted and sent as an image, and the open\nquestion today is what the bar should mean.\n\n## Defaults must show\nA believable meet for a swimmer who does not exist, at 1080x1080:\n\n| event | entry | final | drop | standard |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 50 Free | 31.84 | 30.97 | -0.87 | BB |\n| 100 Free | 1:10.52 | 1:08.31 | -2.21 | BB |\n| 200 Free | 2:36.40 | 2:29.85 | -6.55 | B |\n| 100 Back | 1:19.77 | 1:20.19 | +0.42 | |\n| 50 Fly | 36.10 | 34.92 | -1.18 | B |\n| 200 IM | 2:58.03 | 2:54.02 | -4.01 | |\n\nSummary: `5 of 6 swims faster`, `14.82 s dropped`. A stranger sees a swim\nmeet result for one child: six events, two times each, five drops with bars,\none honest slower swim, four standard chips.\n\nThe rows were chosen so the two variants disagree visibly: in percent the 50\nFree (2.7%) is a longer bar than the 200 IM (2.3%); in seconds the IM (4.01)\nis more than four times the 50 Free (0.87).\n\nThe defaults name no age group or sex: the chips are illustrative and have\nnot been checked against any season's standards table, so the card must not\nimply one.\n\n## Acceptance rubric (the critic scores against this)\n1. Renders at defaults at 1080x1080, 1920x1080 and 1080x1920, exit 0, no\n   error mosaic, and the square still reads to a stranger as a swimmer's\n   meet: events, entry and final times, drops, chips, the summary.\n2. The arithmetic is honest: integer hundredths, drop = entry - final, the\n   summary's count and total as defined above (the defaults give 5 of 6 and\n   14.82). A slower swim shows a plus sign, no bar and no warning colour;\n   NT and DQ rows make no claim. `vs entry time` is on the card; the words\n   \"PB\" and \"best time\" are nowhere on it.\n3. The bar says what the footnote says: bar lengths are proportional to the\n   stated quantity (a unit test checks them against the numbers, within the\n   pixel or two quantization takes), the largest fills its track, all tracks\n   share one size and left edge, and `bar` switches the lengths and the\n   footnote together.\n4. Nothing clips or collides from 1 to 8 rows at the seven contract\n   canvases, with a 30-character swimmer name, `100 Breaststroke`, a distance\n   swim (`19:58.44`, a drop of `-1:02.33`), `AAAA` chips on every row, and\n   emptied club / meet / details lines. One or two rows do not become slabs.\n5. The props are a results row and nothing more: no field a parsed results\n   file would not hold except `standard`. The ship note shows how parsed\n   rows map onto `swims`, and neither it nor the tutorial claims the\n   template reads `.hy3` / `.cl2`.\n\nAlso for the builder (the critic's own line 9 will look): the scout's\nevidence quotes real children by name. The why-tutorial's problem page\nquotes the recaps with the names removed (\"[swimmer] dropped 40 seconds in\nthe 200 Free!\"), following day 8's ruling that a tutorial card does not\ncarry a private person's name.\n\n## Variants worth trying (up to 3, each ONE idea different)\n- a: `bar: \"percent\"`. The bar is the share of the entry time dropped; fair\n  between a 50 and a 200. Footnote along the lines of `vs entry time. Bars:\n  share of the entry time dropped; the longest is 4.2%.`\n- b: `bar: \"seconds\"`. The bar is the seconds dropped, the scout's sketch\n  and the recaps' own unit; the long events dominate. Footnote names the\n  longest in seconds.\n- No c is asked for. If a and b are both clean and there is time, the one\n  further idea worth a render is variant a with each bar's percent printed\n  at the bar's end, to test whether a bar in percent beside a number in\n  seconds confuses without it. Drop it if any label fails to fit.\n\n## What is weak in this plan\n- The template does not read the results file. Someone has to parse `.hy3`\n  (flipturn does) and look up standards before the batch works; the day\n  delivers the card, not the pipeline.\n- Eight rows is a cap, and a three-day championship meet with prelims and\n  finals can exceed it. The error says so; it does not solve it.\n- Relays are out: a relay result has no entry time per swimmer.\n- A bar in percent next to a number in seconds asks the reader to hold two\n  units. The footnote is the only thing that explains it; that is the risk\n  variant b exists to measure.\n","numLines":240,"startLine":1,"totalLines":240}}}
